CAT = "fladjWorkComp"; SEG = "WCP"
Q = [
("A Florida private employer outside the construction industry must provide workers' compensation once it has:",
 "4 or more employees", ["1 or more employees", "10 or more employees", "6 or more employees"],
 "s. 440.02 defines employment to include private employment with four or more employees, or one or more employees in the construction industry."),
("A Florida construction contractor must provide workers' compensation when it has:",
 "1 or more employees", ["4 or more employees", "3 or more employees", "5 or more employees"],
 "In the construction industry, s. 440.02 covers all private employment with one or more employees; outside construction the threshold is four."),
("Florida workers' compensation pays nothing for the first 7 days of disability unless the disability lasts more than:",
 "21 days", ["14 days", "30 days", "10 days"],
 "s. 440.12(1) allows no compensation for the first 7 days, but if disability lasts more than 21 days, compensation is paid from the start. Medical benefits are not subject to the waiting period."),
("Florida temporary total disability benefits are generally paid at:",
 "66 2/3% of the average weekly wage", ["80% of the weekly wage loss", "60% of the worker's average weekly wage", "70% of gross weekly earnings"],
 "s. 440.15(2)(a) pays 66 2/3 percent of the average weekly wage during temporary total disability, subject to the statewide maximum. The 80 percent rate applies only to listed catastrophic injuries."),
("An injured Florida worker must tell the employer about a work injury within:",
 "30 days", ["7 days", "90 days", "1 year"],
 "s. 440.185(1) requires notice within 30 days after the injury or its initial manifestation, with exceptions such as the employer's actual knowledge."),
("After learning of a work injury, a Florida employer must report it to its workers' compensation carrier within:",
 "7 days", ["3 days", "14 days", "30 days"],
 "s. 440.185(2) requires the employer to report the injury to its carrier within 7 days after actual knowledge and give the employee a copy."),
("Within 3 business days after learning of an injury, a Florida workers' compensation carrier must send the worker:",
 "An informational brochure", ["The first benefit check", "A denial or acceptance", "A list of medical bills"],
 "s. 440.185(3) requires the carrier to mail or e-mail the department-approved brochure explaining the worker's rights, benefits and obligations."),
("A Florida carrier that starts paying benefits while it investigates must admit or deny compensability within:",
 "120 days", ["60 days", "90 days", "180 days"],
 "Under s. 440.20(4), a carrier that pays pending investigation must admit or deny within 120 days after the initial provision of benefits, or it waives the right to deny, with narrow exceptions."),
("In Florida workers' compensation, the work accident must be the major contributing cause of the injury, which means:",
 "More than 50 percent responsible", ["At least 25 percent responsible", "Responsible in any part", "The only possible cause"],
 "s. 440.09(1) defines major contributing cause as the cause more than 50 percent responsible for the injury compared with all other causes combined."),
("Florida workers' compensation benefits are not payable if the injury was caused primarily by:",
 "The worker's intoxication", ["A coworker's carelessness", "The employer's negligence", "A missing machine guard"],
 "s. 440.09(3) bars compensation for an injury occasioned primarily by the employee's intoxication, unprescribed drugs, or willful intent to injure. Coworker or employer fault does not matter in a no-fault system."),
("An injured Florida worker who wants a different authorized doctor is entitled, on written request, to:",
 "One change of physician", ["Unlimited changes of doctor", "No change of doctor at all", "Two changes in each year"],
 "s. 440.13 gives the employee one change of physician during treatment for any one accident, on written request to the carrier."),
("Florida temporary partial disability pays 80 percent of the difference between 80 percent of the average weekly wage and:",
 "What the worker earns now", ["The statewide average wage", "The prior year's earnings", "The temporary total rate"],
 "s. 440.15(4)(a) compares 80 percent of the pre-injury average weekly wage with post-injury earnings, pays 80 percent of the gap, and caps the benefit at 66 2/3 percent of the wage."),
]
